+# Super FX bus arbitration — status and implementation boundary
+
+Date: 2026-11-23
+Baseline: SNESticleRevive SuperFX Integration Pass2
+
+## Important status
+
+**This package does not contain a completed GSU-side WAIT/stall implementation.**
+The latest investigation identified the missing work and specified the required
+architecture, but no source-code change implementing that architecture was made.
+This document is deliberately explicit so this source snapshot is not mistaken
+for a completed bus-arbitration release.
+
+## What the baseline already implements
+
+- S-CPU ROM reads can be trapped and synchronized with the GSU.
+- When the GSU is running and owns ROM (`GO=1`, `SCMR.RON=1`), the S-CPU ROM
+  read path returns the address-dependent Super FX snooze/busy vector.
+- CPU-side Game Pak RAM ownership checks exist in the SRAM access path.
+- GSU master-clock synchronization and buffered ROM/RAM access timing exist in
+  the current instruction-granular core.
+- Pass2 save states preserve the GSU state and `GSULineClock`, with V3 format
+  and backward handling for V2/legacy states.
+
+These features are not equivalent to complete, cycle-accurate arbitration.
+
+## Confirmed remaining gaps
+
+The GSU-side memory accessors currently complete accesses directly rather than
+suspending and resuming an instruction when the required bus is not granted:
+
+- `SNGSU::RomReadByte()`
+- `SNGSU::ProgramReadByte()` / `RawCodeRead()` / `CodeRead()`
+- `SNGSU::RamReadByte()`
+- `SNGSU::RamWriteByte()`
+- ROM prefetch and code-cache fill paths
+- buffered RAM writes and graphics-related RAM reads such as `RPIX`
+
+A correct implementation must cover all of these paths, not just the four
+byte-access helpers. It must also preserve instruction and pipeline state when
+an access stalls, and must serialize pending bus operations in save states.
+
+## Required architecture for a future complete implementation
+
+1. A central arbiter tracks ROM and RAM ownership independently and exposes
+   deterministic grant/wait results to both processors.
+2. GSU execution becomes resumable at memory-access boundaries. A blocked
+   instruction must retain its micro-operation, operands, pipeline state,
+   and already-completed side effects; it must not restart the instruction.
+3. The scheduler advances the current bus owner while the requester waits,
+   accounting for GSU clock selection and master-clock conversion.
+4. Instruction fetch, cache-line fill, prefetch, data reads, buffered writes,
+   `PLOT`, and `RPIX` all use the same arbitration rules.
+5. Save-state V4 (or a compatible extension) records arbiter ownership,
+   pending transactions, remaining wait time, and resumable instruction state.
+6. Regression tests cover RON/RAN transitions, GO/STOP, simultaneous requests,
+   cache hits/misses, slow/fast GSU clock, save/restore during a stall, and
+   deterministic replay.
+
+## Why a simple patch is incorrect
+
+Returning `0xFF`, returning open bus, adding a fixed delay, or blocking inside a
+memory accessor without a resumable execution model is not a correct WAIT
+implementation. Such shortcuts either execute with invalid data, deadlock the
+emulator, or repeat instruction side effects after resumption.
+
+## Validation status
+
+The Pass2 baseline has its own build and host-test records. Those results do
+**not** validate the missing full-system GSU-side WAIT/stall behavior. This
+package makes no claim that the Star Fox black-screen issue is fixed, and it
+has not been runtime-validated as a new arbitration build in PCSX2 or on PS2.
+
+## Relevant source files
+
+- `src/snes/core/sngsu.h`
+- `src/snes/core/sngsu.cpp`
+- `src/snes/snes.cpp` (CPU/GSU synchronization and bus integration)
+- `src/snes/state/snstate.h`
+- `src/snes/state/snstate.cpp`
+
+The source files above are the Pass2 baseline; they are not represented as
+containing the future architecture described in this document.
