+# Super FX Integration Pass 2 — scheduler-safe save states
+
+## Scope
+
+This pass closes a scheduler-state gap found while reviewing the Pass 1 integration. The GSU's internal state was serialized, but the S-CPU/GSU line-clock rendezvous (`m_nGSULineClock`) was not.
+
+## Change
+
+- Added `SnesStateV3T`, retaining the complete V2 layout as a separate compatibility structure.
+- V3 stores the GSU's current master-clock position within the scanline alongside the GSU core state.
+- `GetStateSize()` now reports V3.
+- V3 restore restores the saved GSU timestamp, clamped to the current NTSC line-clock range.
+- V2 restore remains supported: because V2 did not contain the GSU timestamp, it re-anchors the GSU timestamp to the restored S-CPU line counter.
+- Legacy `SnesStateT` restore remains supported and resets the GSU scheduler timestamp to zero after resetting/reconnecting the GSU.
+
+This prevents a mid-scanline state restore from replaying GSU clocks already consumed earlier in that line, or skipping the remaining interval due to a stale scheduler timestamp.
+
+## Validation
+
+- PS2 EE build with `SNES_DIAGNOSTICS=2`: PASS; link and strip completed.
+- GSU regression suite: PASS; 17,960 fuzz-oracle cases, zero failures.
+- Bitplane regression: PASS; 98,304 cases.
+- GSU core state round-trip: PASS.
+- PS2 ELF was inserted into the existing Pass 1 Star Fox ISO. The ISO directory entry file length was updated, and the ELF payload was byte-for-byte verified after insertion. The new ELF occupies the same 2,646 ISO sectors as the previous ELF.
+
+## Not validated
+
+- No PCSX2 run or physical-PS2 run was performed in this environment.
+- No test yet saves/restores the complete `SnesSystem` in the middle of a scanline and compares execution against an uninterrupted run. The EE build confirms the V3 serialization code compiles, but not its runtime determinism.
+- This pass does not claim to solve Star Fox's black-screen issue. It closes the save-state/scheduler timestamp gap and preserves the existing CPU/GSU synchronization and bus-arbitration behavior.
