+# SuperFX RAM write buffer — Stage 2
+
+Date: 2026-11-23
+
+## Scope
+
+This change adds the delayed Game Pak RAM write buffer to the existing clean-room
+GSU implementation. It does not import or adapt source code from Snes9x or another
+emulator. Public hardware documentation and independently observable behavior are
+used as references.
+
+## Model
+
+- The GSU has one pending RAM-write slot.
+- Enqueueing a write first drains any older pending write.
+- The new write is committed after 5 GSU clocks with CLSR fast, or 6 clocks
+  with CLSR slow.
+- The pending address is stored as a linear RAM offset when enqueued, so a later
+  RAMBR change cannot redirect an already-issued write.
+- RAM reads are not buffered. Before a read, the pending write is drained.
+- RAMB waits for the pending write before changing the selected RAM bank.
+- The write buffer continues to receive clocks after GO becomes zero. This keeps
+  the final write of a job from being lost when STOP executes before writeback.
+
+## Integrated operations
+
+The buffer is used by STB, STW, SM, SMS, SBK, and pixel-cache writeback from PLOT.
+Loads (LDB, LDW, LM, LMS, RPIX and partial-pixel read/modify/write) drain an older
+write before reading RAM.
+
+Word stores are represented as two byte writes through the single-entry buffer.
+The second byte therefore waits for the first byte to finish, while the final byte
+may remain pending as later GSU instructions execute.
+
+## Clock progression
+
+`AdvanceClocks()` services both the ROM read buffer and RAM write buffer. The
+scheduler continues to call `RunCycles()` while the GSU is stopped, so pending
+writeback can complete after STOP. The legacy instruction-count test helper drains
+the final pending write when it reaches STOP.
+
+## Regression coverage
+
+`tools/superfxtest/gsu_test.cpp` includes a cache-executed STB test which checks:
+
+1. the GSU reaches STOP while the write is still pending;
+2. the RAM byte remains unchanged after STOP and after three additional clocks;
+3. the byte is committed on the fourth additional clock (the first clock was
+   consumed by the STOP instruction fetch, out of a five-clock fast write delay).
+
+Existing STW/LDW, SM/LM, PLOT/RPIX, bitplane and instruction fuzz tests are retained.
+
+## Validation
+
+- `tools/superfxtest/build.sh`: PASS
+- `gsu_test`: PASS, 17,960 fuzz-oracle cases, zero failures
+- `planes_test`: PASS, 98,304 cases
+- Host build with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer: PASS
+- PS2 cross-compilation and physical-console validation: NOT RUN in this environment
+
+## Known limits
+
+This is a first RAM-buffer timing model, not cycle-perfect arbitration. It does not
+yet model every interaction between the SNES CPU and the GSU's RAN ownership,
+external SRAM/backup RAM, or all pixel-cache/bus contention corner cases. The
+scheduler is still batched at scanline granularity. These remain separate tasks.
