@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# SuperFX Stage 8 — GSU program ROM decode and BRAMR clarification
+
+## Changes
+
+- Corrected the GSU-side LoROM decode: banks `$00-$3F` expose ROM only at `$8000-$FFFF`; `$0000-$7FFF` now reads as unmapped (`$FF`) rather than aliasing the upper half.
+- Kept the complete `$0000-$FFFF` window for banks `$40-$5F`, the GSU's linear ROM window.
+- Added deterministic regression tests for both sides of this mapping boundary.
+- Clarified `$3033` BRAMR handling. It controls a separate backup-RAM device at `$78-$79`; it does not protect normal Game Pak RAM at `$70-$71`. The supported SuperFX boards in this core do not expose a separate BRAM device, so the write remains a no-op rather than incorrectly gating Game Pak RAM.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Host GSU tests: PASS, including 17,960 fuzz-oracle cases, zero failures.
+- Bitplane tests: PASS, 98,304 cases.
+- GSU save-state round-trip: PASS.
+- PS2DEV cross-build: all translation units compiled; final ELF link and strip succeeded; zero build errors. The build was resumed after the execution window interrupted the first link attempt.
+
+## Important remaining work
+
+This stage is not a complete, cycle-accurate SuperFX implementation. Still open:
+
+1. GSU-side ROM/RAM bus wait/stall behavior when RON/RAN are not granted.
+2. Per-opcode cycle timing and memory/cache wait validation against trusted test ROMs or hardware.
+3. Fine-grained interleaving of GSU events with S-CPU bus/MMIO events; the current scheduler remains scanline-sliced and instruction-atomic.
+4. Full validation of PLOT/RPIX, pixel-cache edge cases, and rendering against reference output.
+5. Validation of all opcode variants, pipeline/delay-slot edge cases, IRQ behavior, and all supported GSU revisions.
+6. Runtime gameplay validation on PCSX2 and physical PS2. No Star Fox gameplay claim is made from host tests or a successful cross-build.
+
+BRAMR is documented as a separate-chip control and is intentionally not treated as a Game Pak RAM enable bit. A future board implementation may add a distinct backup-RAM device if a supported cartridge actually exposes one.
